<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helsingborg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Helsingborg is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, on the narrowest point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a> opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Danish for most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, its castle guarded the Sound, and in 1362 Valdemar IV defeated a Hanseatic fleet off the town. It passed to Sweden in 1658, and in 1710 a Swedish army under Magnus Stenbock defeated a Danish invasion force at the Battle of Helsingborg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helsingborg.jpg" alt="Helsingborg" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This image was produced by me, David Castor (dcastor). The pictures I submit to the Wikipedia Project are released to the public domain. … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inre_hamnen_i_Helsingborg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Helsingborg stands at the narrowest part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a>, the strait between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, about 4 km from the Danish city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> on the opposite shore. It is one of the oldest cities of what is now Sweden and has been the site of permanent settlement officially since 21 May 1085. Its position was very important for Denmark, which at that time controlled both sides of the strait.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1429 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a> introduced the Sound Dues, a levy on trading vessels passing through the sound between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> and Helsingborg. The dues were one of the main sources of income for the Danish Crown. They were first directed against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, and crossing traffic such as fishermen did not pay them.</p><p>After the Dano-Swedish War of 1657 to 1658 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> had to give up all its territory on the southern Scandinavian peninsula, and Helsingborg became Swedish. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> landed there on 5 March 1658 to take personal possession of the Scanian lands, and a delegation led by Peder Winstrup, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a>, met him. The town then had barely 1,000 people. Denmark recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> twice but could not hold it, and the last attempt came in 1710, when 14,000 men landed near Helsingborg. The Battle of Helsingborg was fought just outside the city on 10 March, and the town recovered slowly, with only 1,321 inhabitants in 1770.</p><p>From the middle of the 19th century industrialisation made Helsingborg one of the fastest-growing Swedish cities, rising from 4,000 people in 1850 to 20,000 in 1890 and 56,000 in 1930. A train ferry linked it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> from 1892. In October 1943 it was one of the main landing points for Danish Jews, over 7,200 of whom, with 680 non-Jews, were smuggled across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in three nights.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Helsingborg is the seat of its municipality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, the second-largest city of that province after Malmö, with a population of 151,404 in 2024. Its medieval fortress, Kärnan, about 600 years old, stands in the city centre among old stone churches. Ferries still cross to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> around the clock, and IKEA has its international corporate headquarters in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helsingborg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The great test came when Valdemar IV of Denmark sacked Visby in 1361 and defeated a Hanseatic fleet at <b>Helsingborg</b> in 1362.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Denmark re-entered the war and invaded Scania, though it was defeated at <b>Helsingborg</b> in 1710.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
